1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,860
Hi, I'm Chris.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,260
I've spent over 10 years

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:06,220
in data engineering

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,100
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,860
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

6
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,020
the skills that actually get you hired.

7
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:14,560
Let's get into it.

8
00:00:15,097 --> 00:00:16,317
Every morning,

9
00:00:16,337 --> 00:00:18,757
before anyone at work opens a laptop,

10
00:00:18,777 --> 00:00:19,797
thousands of data

11
00:00:19,817 --> 00:00:21,877
pipelines have already run.

12
00:00:21,897 --> 00:00:23,077
Something decided when

13
00:00:23,097 --> 00:00:24,437
each one should start,

14
00:00:24,457 --> 00:00:25,477
in what order,

15
00:00:25,497 --> 00:00:27,637
and what to do when one fails.

16
00:00:27,657 --> 00:00:29,037
In a lot of companies,

17
00:00:29,057 --> 00:00:31,637
that something is Apache Airflow.

18
00:00:31,657 --> 00:00:33,397
Airflow is an open-source tool

19
00:00:33,417 --> 00:00:35,317
for orchestrating workflows.

20
00:00:35,337 --> 00:00:36,677
Orchestrating just means

21
00:00:36,697 --> 00:00:37,917
running the right task,

22
00:00:37,937 --> 00:00:40,277
at the right time, in the right order.

23
00:00:40,297 --> 00:00:42,917
It started at Airbnb in 2014,

24
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:45,797
and today it's a top-level Apache project.

25
00:00:45,817 --> 00:00:46,997
You write each pipeline

26
00:00:47,017 --> 00:00:49,237
in Python, as a DAG.

27
00:00:49,257 --> 00:00:52,357
DAG just means directed acyclic graph.

28
00:00:52,377 --> 00:00:55,597
Each box is a task: extract the orders,

29
00:00:55,617 --> 00:00:56,357
transform them,

30
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:58,197
and load them into the warehouse.

31
00:00:58,217 --> 00:01:00,037
The arrows are dependencies,

32
00:01:00,057 --> 00:01:01,077
so a task only

33
00:01:01,097 --> 00:01:03,957
runs after the tasks before it succeed.

34
00:01:03,977 --> 00:01:05,477
Now, the architecture.

35
00:01:05,497 --> 00:01:08,077
The DAG processor reads your Python files

36
00:01:08,097 --> 00:01:09,517
and saves each DAG

37
00:01:09,537 --> 00:01:11,237
to the metadata database.

38
00:01:11,257 --> 00:01:12,717
The scheduler checks that

39
00:01:12,737 --> 00:01:14,117
database, finds the tasks

40
00:01:14,137 --> 00:01:14,944
that are ready,

41
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:16,757
and hands them to the executor.

42
00:01:16,777 --> 00:01:18,877
The executor sends them out to workers,

43
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:20,997
and the workers do the actual work.

44
00:01:21,017 --> 00:01:22,877
Every result — queued,

45
00:01:22,897 --> 00:01:24,597
running, success or failed

46
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:27,037
— goes back into the metadata database.

47
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:28,837
And the web UI sits on top,

48
00:01:28,857 --> 00:01:30,557
so you can see every run,

49
00:01:30,577 --> 00:01:31,397
read the logs,

50
00:01:31,417 --> 00:01:34,170
and retry a failed task with one click.

51
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:36,677
So when a team says, "we use Airflow,"

52
00:01:36,697 --> 00:01:38,997
it means their pipelines are code.

53
00:01:39,017 --> 00:01:40,157
They run on a schedule,

54
00:01:40,177 --> 00:01:41,237
in the right order,

55
00:01:41,257 --> 00:01:42,517
and when something breaks,

56
00:01:42,537 --> 00:01:44,357
they know exactly where.

57
00:01:44,377 --> 00:01:46,457
That's Apache Airflow.

58
00:01:48,517 --> 00:01:50,657
So, what should I explain next?

59
00:01:50,677 --> 00:01:51,817
Tell me in the comments below,

60
00:01:51,837 --> 00:01:54,037
and it could be the next episode.
